1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,760
The name Grotta del Buemarino comes from the presence until just a few decades ago

2
00:00:05,760 --> 00:00:12,640
of Monicus Monicus, the Mediterranean monk seal known in Sardinian as Sue Oe Marino,

3
00:00:12,640 --> 00:00:19,600
the sea ox or sea bull. The nickname derives from its massive build and the deep sounds it produced

4
00:00:19,600 --> 00:00:26,160
inside the coastal cavities. A call that amplified by the cave walls was reminiscent of the lowing

5
00:00:26,160 --> 00:00:31,920
of an ox. The Mediterranean monk seal is one of the rarest marine mammals in the world

6
00:00:31,920 --> 00:00:38,400
and is a species on the verge of extinction. It is estimated that no more than 500 individuals

7
00:00:38,400 --> 00:00:44,480
remain across the entire Mediterranean Sea, concentrated mainly in a few particularly

8
00:00:44,480 --> 00:00:50,960
isolated and protected areas such as specific Greek archipelagos and stretches of the North

9
00:00:50,960 --> 00:00:58,560
African coast. Its presence today is fragmented and precarious, tied to places that remain largely

10
00:00:58,560 --> 00:01:06,480
untouched by human activity. An adult specimen can reach 2.5 meters in length and an average

11
00:01:06,480 --> 00:01:13,600
weight of between 250 and 300 kilograms. The body is sturdy, the snout short and rounded,

12
00:01:13,600 --> 00:01:20,960
and the eyes large and dark. The coat varies from gray to dark brown. Until the 1980s,

13
00:01:20,960 --> 00:01:27,200
the monk seal lived permanently in the Gulf of Orise and used these caves as a safe refuge

14
00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:33,200
for resting and giving birth. With the growth of tourism, nautical traffic and human presence

15
00:01:33,200 --> 00:01:39,600
along the coast, the species gradually abandoned the area and eventually disappeared. It was not,

16
00:01:39,680 --> 00:01:46,720
however, an animal particularly appreciated by local fishermen. Attracted to fish caught in nets,

17
00:01:46,720 --> 00:01:53,120
it would tear through them and easily feed on the catch, causing economic damage. This conflict

18
00:01:53,120 --> 00:02:00,080
with humans contributed over time to its persecution. Today, its memory lives on in the name of this

19
00:02:00,080 --> 00:02:06,480
cave. Walking through here amid silence and crystal clear water, we can imagine a time

20
00:02:06,480 --> 00:02:11,920
when this place was the refuge of one of the most fascinating, rare and vulnerable animals in the

21
00:02:11,920 --> 00:02:13,120
Mediterranean.

